id: contracts-01
courseId: frontend-architecture
title: "Contracts between ==teams=="
concepts: [remote-contract, contract-types, contract-versioning, contract-detection]
# Sources for every claim about tools (checked 2026-10-06; quoted text was read on the linked page that day).
# Claim register: content/frontend-architecture/sources.md. Runtime output: samples/recordings.json (see below).
# - exposes: "Determines the modules and file entries exposed by Module Federation"; the type is an object of
#   [exposeKey: string] entries and the example exposes several keys ('.', './add', './Button') at once:
#   https://module-federation.io/configure/exposes.html
# - React lazy: React "will wait for it to resolve, and then render the resolved value's .default as a React
#   component"; load "needs to eventually resolve to an object whose .default property is a valid React component
#   type"; "If the Promise rejects, React will throw the rejection reason for the nearest Error Boundary":
#   https://react.dev/reference/react/lazy
# - React error 306, full text: "Element type is invalid. Received a promise that resolves to: %s. Lazy element type
#   must resolve to a class or function.":
#   https://react.dev/errors/306
# - Troubleshooting RUNTIME-014, "The remote does not expose the requested module"; causes include "The producer build
#   config does not declare this expose". Our runtime printed a different text for this case (see recordings), so the
#   lesson quotes our recorded text and does not claim the code RUNTIME-014 appears:
#   https://module-federation.io/guide/troubleshooting/runtime.html
# - dts: default true; "the producer will automatically generate a compressed type file @mf-types.zip (default name)
#   during build, and the consumer will automatically pull the type file of remotes and decompress it to @mf-types";
#   typesOnBuild: "By default, Module Federation does not load type files in production (process.env.NODE_ENV ===
#   'production'). To enable type loading in production builds, set typesOnBuild to true." Default false:
#   https://module-federation.io/configure/dts.html
# - Type hinting: "@module-federation/enhanced has type hinting enabled by default"; the consumer adds tsconfig paths
#   "*": ["./@mf-types/*"]; "After modifying the producer's code, the consumer will automatically fetch the producer's
#   types" (type hot reloading, a dev feature):
#   https://module-federation.io/guide/basic/type-prompt.html
# - Manifest: "mf-manifest.json is a runtime manifest generated by a producer after build. It describes which modules
#   the producer exposes, where the remote entry is, ... and where the type files are":
#   https://module-federation.io/guide/basic/manifest-snapshot.html
# Recordings from our example project (examples/host, remote-a, remote-b, built with "pnpm examples:build"; remote_a or
# the host changed in scratch builds that were never committed; pages loaded with the jsdom loader of
# examples/recorder/src/record.ts, fresh page, nothing cached, three runs with identical results). They are not in
# recorded/outputs.json, which this lesson may not edit. All of them are in samples/recordings.json.
# - rename-expose.ts, drop-default.ts, which-ships-alone.ts, release-order.ts: the pages of each host and remote_a pair.
# - host-typecheck.ts: tsc on the host with its own src/remotes.d.ts, exit 0.
# - published-types.ts, which-ships-alone.ts: tsc on host/src/App.tsx with paths pointing at the @mf-types.zip of each
#   remote_a build (TS2307 for the rename, TS2322 for the deleted default export, exit 0 otherwise).
# - Our host build ("rsbuild build", production) wrote no @mf-types folder and printed no type download.
# - check-contract.ts was run on the mf-manifest.json of every remote_a build: "contract broken, missing: ./Widget" for
#   the rename and for the build that exposes only './Card'; "check passed" for all others, including the deleted
#   default export (check-rename.ts and check-drop-default.ts show these two results).
# Labelled as ours, not as documented features: the two pre-deploy checks (manifest names, compiling hosts against the
# new @mf-types.zip) and the add-then-remove release order. The versioning step is a trade-off, labelled as such.
---

{% hook id="h1" estSeconds=40 character="bug" concepts=["remote-contract"] %}
Tuesday, 11:40. The Remote a team tidies up: `./Widget` becomes `./Card` in their `exposes`. Their typecheck is green, their own page works, they deploy. The Host team is at lunch and rebuilds nothing. The Bug has read the host's code and is smiling.
{% margin %}
{% stop %}
Who else uses the name ./Widget, and does remote_a's CI know about them?
{% /stop %}
{% /margin %}
{% /hook %}

{% predict id="p1" estSeconds=60 code="./samples/rename-expose.ts" language="ts" concepts=["remote-contract"] %}
{% option output="Remote a | Remote b" misconception="remote-exports-change-freely" %}
The rename felt internal to remote_a, and its own checks passed. But the host asks for ./Widget by name at runtime, and that name is gone.
{% /option %}
{% option output="remote_a failed: Module \"./Widget\" does not exist in container. | Remote b" correct=true %}
Recorded from our example. The host still asked for ./Widget. Its error boundary caught the failure, so Remote b kept working.
{% /option %}
{% margin %}
{% sticky label="asks" %}
Their CI was green. Whose CI should have gone red?
{% /sticky %}
{% /margin %}
{% /predict %}

{% explain id="e1" estSeconds=60 code="./samples/contract.ts" concepts=["remote-contract"] %}
The host depends on two things remote_a owns: the **expose name** in `exposes` ([docs](https://module-federation.io/configure/exposes.html)) and the shape of the module behind it. `lazy` renders the resolved module's `.default` ([React docs](https://react.dev/reference/react/lazy)), so the default export belongs to the deal too. That pair is the contract. Nothing in remote_a's build reads the host, so remote_a's own checks cannot see it.

{% annotation line=4 %}
The name the host asks for.
{% /annotation %}
{% annotation line=7 %}
lazy() renders exactly this export.
{% /annotation %}
{% annotation line=9 %}
Host side: expose name, then default export.
{% /annotation %}
{% /explain %}

{% predict id="p2" estSeconds=60 code="./samples/drop-default.ts" language="ts" concepts=["remote-contract", "contract-types"] %}
{% option output="Remote a | Remote b" misconception="remote-exports-change-freely" %}
The expose name did not change, so loading worked. Rendering did not: lazy found no default export to render.
{% /option %}
{% option output="remote_a failed: Minified React error #306 | Remote b" correct=true %}
Recorded from our example. React's page for error 306 says a lazy element type must resolve to a class or function. Here it resolved to undefined.
{% /option %}
{% option output="remote_a failed: Module \"./Widget\" does not exist in container. | Remote b" misconception="remote-exports-change-freely" %}
./Widget is still in exposes, so the module loaded. The break came one step later, when React looked for its default export.
{% /option %}
{% /predict %}

{% fillBlanks id="f1" estSeconds=60 template="// remote_a/src/Widget.tsx: the export the host renders\nexport ___kw___ Widget;\n\n// host/src/App.tsx\nconst RemoteA = lazy(() => import('remote_a/___name___'));" language="ts" concepts=["remote-contract"] %}
{% blank id="kw" accepted=["default"] misconception="remote-exports-change-freely" %}
lazy renders the module's default export. Delete that line and the host gets undefined, as in the recording.
{% /blank %}
{% blank id="name" accepted=["Widget"] misconception="remote-exports-change-freely" %}
After the slash comes the expose name without ./. Rename it in exposes and this import breaks at runtime.
{% /blank %}
{% /fillBlanks %}

{% predict id="p3" estSeconds=45 code="./samples/host-typecheck.ts" language="ts" concepts=["contract-types"] %}
{% option output="tsc exit 0" correct=true %}
Recorded. The host checks against its own remotes.d.ts, a copy of the contract written by hand. The copy still promises a default export.
{% /option %}
{% option output="tsc exit 2: TS2322" misconception="remote-exports-change-freely" %}
That error needs the remote's new types. This typecheck never looks at them; the host's types are its own file.
{% /option %}
{% option output="tsc exit 2: TS2307" misconception="remote-exports-change-freely" %}
TS2307 means a module was not found. The host's own declaration of remote_a/Widget is still there.
{% /option %}
{% margin %}
{% sticky label="asks" %}
So the host's types are green while its page is broken?
{% /sticky %}
{% /margin %}
{% /predict %}

{% pitfall id="pf1" estSeconds=45 concepts=["contract-types"] %}
Types describe a contract at one moment. Module Federation can generate them: the remote's build writes `@mf-types.zip`, and a consumer pulls it into `@mf-types` ([dts docs](https://module-federation.io/configure/dts.html), [type hints](https://module-federation.io/guide/basic/type-prompt.html)). By default it does not load them in production builds (`typesOnBuild` is false), and our host build pulled nothing. A remote that deploys after your build changes nothing in your types.
{% margin %}
{% bubble who="bug" %}
Your types are a photo. I deployed after the photo was taken.
{% /bubble %}
{% gotcha %}
Green host types prove only that the host matches its own copy of the contract.
{% /gotcha %}
{% /margin %}
{% /pitfall %}

{% predict id="p4" estSeconds=60 code="./samples/published-types.ts" language="ts" concepts=["contract-types", "contract-detection"] %}
{% option output="tsc exit 0" misconception="remote-exports-change-freely" %}
This time the host compiles against what remote_a really published, and that file has no default export.
{% /option %}
{% option output="tsc exit 2: TS2307 at App.tsx line 4" misconception="remote-exports-change-freely" %}
TS2307 is module not found. We recorded it for the exposes rename. Here ./Widget still exists; only its default export is gone.
{% /option %}
{% option output="tsc exit 2: TS2322 at App.tsx line 4" correct=true %}
Recorded. lazy needs a Promise of an object whose default is a component. The new types offer none, so the check fails before anyone deploys.
{% /option %}
{% /predict %}

{% explain id="e2" estSeconds=60 code="./samples/check-contract.ts" concepts=["contract-detection"] %}
Two checks can run in remote_a's CI after its build, before deploy. Both are ours, not built-in features. One reads the new `mf-manifest.json`, which [describes which modules the producer exposes](https://module-federation.io/guide/basic/manifest-snapshot.html), and fails if a name the host imports is gone. The other compiles each host against the new `@mf-types.zip`, as in the last step. Both need the list of what hosts use.

{% annotation line=4 %}
Kept by the host team, not remote_a.
{% /annotation %}
{% annotation line=8 %}
Names only, such as ./Widget.
{% /annotation %}
{% annotation line=12 %}
Red CI here, before users see anything.
{% /annotation %}
{% /explain %}

{% fillBlanks id="f2" estSeconds=60 template="const manifest = JSON.parse(readFileSync('dist/mf-manifest.json', 'utf8'));\nconst exposed = manifest.___field___.map((e) => e.___prop___);\nconst missing = ['./Widget'].filter((p) => !exposed.includes(p));\nif (missing.length > 0) process.exit(1);" language="ts" concepts=["contract-detection"] %}
{% blank id="field" accepted=["exposes"] misconception="remote-exports-change-freely" %}
The manifest lists the modules a remote exposes under exposes, the same word as in the config.
{% /blank %}
{% blank id="prop" accepted=["path"] misconception="remote-exports-change-freely" %}
Each entry of exposes has a path such as ./Widget. That is the name the host's import asks for.
{% /blank %}
{% /fillBlanks %}

{% predict id="p5" estSeconds=45 code="./samples/check-rename.ts" language="ts" concepts=["contract-detection"] %}
{% option output="check passed" misconception="remote-exports-change-freely" %}
The new manifest exposes only ./Card. The host imports ./Widget, so the list of missing names is not empty.
{% /option %}
{% option output="contract broken, missing: ./Widget" correct=true %}
We ran the script on the real manifest too: same result, exit code 1. remote_a's CI goes red and Tuesday's deploy never ships.
{% /option %}
{% /predict %}

{% predict id="p6" estSeconds=45 code="./samples/check-drop-default.ts" language="ts" concepts=["contract-detection"] %}
{% option output="contract broken, missing: ./Widget" misconception="remote-exports-change-freely" %}
./Widget is still in exposes. The manifest lists names, not what each module exports, so this break slips through.
{% /option %}
{% option output="check passed" correct=true %}
We ran the script on the real manifest too: check passed. The name check catches the rename, not the deleted default export. That one needs the type check.
{% /option %}
{% margin %}
{% sticky label="asks" %}
So the name check alone would wave the second break through?
{% /sticky %}
{% /margin %}
{% /predict %}

{% predict id="p7" estSeconds=60 code="./samples/which-ships-alone.ts" language="ts" concepts=["remote-contract", "contract-versioning"] %}
{% option output="C, D" correct=true %}
Recorded. The function's own name is not part of the contract, its default export is. Adding a name next to the old one breaks nobody.
{% /option %}
{% option output="A, C, D" misconception="remote-exports-change-freely" %}
A looks like a rename inside remote_a, but ./Widget is the name the host imports. The page showed an error box.
{% /option %}
{% option output="B, C, D" misconception="remote-exports-change-freely" %}
B keeps the name ./Widget, but the host renders the default export, and B deleted it. React error 306.
{% /option %}
{% /predict %}

{% explain id="e3" estSeconds=60 code="./samples/versioned-config.ts" concepts=["contract-versioning"] %}
To rename without a joint release, ship the new name next to the old one. `exposes` takes any number of keys ([docs](https://module-federation.io/configure/exposes.html)). Hosts move to `./Card` on their own schedule; `./Widget` goes only when no host imports it. **A trade-off, not a rule:** the remote carries old code for a while. With many hosts deploying on their own, that wins. With one host released together with the remote, one joint release can be simpler.

{% annotation line=5 %}
Old name stays for current hosts.
{% /annotation %}
{% annotation line=6 %}
New name for hosts that move.
{% /annotation %}
{% annotation line=10 %}
A moved host gets the new module.
{% /annotation %}
{% /explain %}

{% fillBlanks id="f3" estSeconds=45 template="// remote_a, release 2. The host still imports remote_a/Widget.\nexposes: {\n  '___old___': './src/Widget.tsx',\n  './Card': './src/Card.tsx',\n}," language="ts" concepts=["contract-versioning"] %}
{% blank id="old" accepted=["./Widget"] misconception="remote-exports-change-freely" %}
Keep the old key, with its ./, until no host imports it. Remove it in a later release, after the hosts have moved.
{% /blank %}
{% /fillBlanks %}

{% recap id="rc1" estSeconds=30 concepts=["remote-contract", "contract-types", "contract-versioning", "contract-detection"] %}
{% point %}
The contract is the ==expose name== plus the export the host uses, here the default export.
{% /point %}
{% point %}
A remote can break the contract with its own build and types green. The host finds out at runtime.
{% /point %}
{% point %}
A host's types are a copy from its own build. Before a remote deploys, check its new manifest and `@mf-types.zip` against every host.
{% /point %}
{% point %}
Rename by adding the new name first. Remove the old one when no host imports it.
{% /point %}
{% margin %}
{% bubble who="bug" %}
It was just a rename, they said. Nobody reads the host, they said.
{% /bubble %}
{% /margin %}
{% /recap %}

{% predict id="p8" estSeconds=60 code="./samples/release-order.ts" language="ts" concepts=["contract-versioning"] %}
{% option output="remote v3, then host v2" misconception="remote-exports-change-freely" %}
Between the two deploys the old host asks for ./Widget, and remote v3 no longer has it. Recorded: an error box.
{% /option %}
{% option output="host v2, then remote v2, then remote v3" misconception="remote-exports-change-freely" %}
Host v2 asks for ./Card before any remote exposes it. Recorded: Module "./Card" does not exist in container.
{% /option %}
{% option output="remote v2, then host v2, then remote v3" correct=true %}
Every state on this path was recorded working. Add, move, then remove: each team deploys alone and the page never breaks.
{% /option %}
{% /predict %}

{% cliffhanger id="c1" estSeconds=20 nextLessonId="routing-01" question="So far the contract is a module name and its default export. But the browser has one address bar. When remote_a wants pages of its own, who decides what a URL shows?" %}
{% margin %}
{% bubble who="bug" %}
Next time two apps fight over one URL. I brought popcorn.
{% /bubble %}
{% /margin %}
{% /cliffhanger %}
